  1030	### 9.2 External `go-repository-v1` receipt schema 2
  1031	
  1032	Each active external command MUST use build receipt schema 2. Its logical input
  1033	MUST contain:
  1034	
  1035	- schema version 2 and driver `go-repository-v1`;
  1036	- repository identifier;
  1037	- declared canonical `network-git` identity, transport, immutable object
  1038	  format and full commit lock, and OPTIONAL tag;
  1039	- effective identity, transport when applicable, actual object format, full
  1040	  commit, `substituted`, typed substitution when present, and external
  1041	  `curator-build-source-v1`;
  1042	- descriptor path fixed to `skill-build.json` and selected target;
  1043	- consuming manifest command name, selected `build_root`, and `source_dir`;
  1044	- native GOOS/GOARCH/tuning and complete `curator-go-toolchain-v1`; and
  1045	- the policy object from section 9.1 plus
  1046	  `"source_kind":"locked-external-git-v1"`.
  1047	
  1048	For `substituted: false`, effective identity, object format, and commit MUST
  1049	equal the declaration and substitution MUST be absent. If declared `tag` is
  1050	present, a writer MUST NOT publish the receipt until that same operation's
  1051	exact-tag acquisition and manager-computed peel prove the terminal commit
  1052	equals the full lock. The receipt MUST NOT contain a self-asserted trust,
  1053	provenance, or `tag_verified` field.
  1054	
  1055	For `substituted: true`, the declaration remains unchanged and effective source
  1056	MUST describe the actual compiled snapshot. A local substitution uses identity
  1057	kind `operator-local-git` and substitution `{"type":"local-path"}`. A network
  1058	substitution uses identity kind `network-git`, canonical value and transport,
  1059	and typed structured ref. A substituted receipt's declared tag is declaration
  1060	history only; it MUST NOT claim that the declared remote tag was consulted.
  1061	
  1062	The schema-2 logical cache key is the SHA-256 of `CCJ-1` over the complete
  1063	input. The receipt MUST retain the complete input, logical cache key,
  1064	manager-derived artifact-relative path, artifact SHA-256, and byte length in
  1065	exact canonical bytes. Different declared or effective states, substitutions,
  1066	commands, targets, build roots, source directories, native targets,
  1067	toolchains, execution policies, or policy revisions MUST NOT alias.
  1068	
  1069	### 9.3 Shared protected-cache rules
  1070	
  1071	Before reuse, a reader MUST open a regular singly linked receipt without
  1072	following links, recanonicalize it, and require exact stored-byte equality;
  1073	recompute the build-source identity before opening the candidate; recompute and
  1074	match the cache key and entire expected input; require the manager-derived
  1075	artifact path; and open, bound, hash, and size-check one regular singly linked
  1076	artifact without following links. It MUST reject unknown receipt fields or
  1077	unsupported receipt, driver, build-source, toolchain, and execution-policy
  1078	identities. An entry whose execution policy is absent or is not the policy the
  1079	reader implements is a miss, never a hit and never an upgrade. It MUST NOT
  1080	execute the artifact.
  1081	
  1082	Persistent reuse is permitted only below manager-created, manager-protected
  1083	state resolved independently of package input. On every lookup and again under
  1084	the manager-home mutation lock, the manager MUST verify ownership, private
  1085	mutation permissions or DACL, containment, regular file types, and link safety
  1086	for the boundary and every entry component. An implementation that cannot
  1087	prove this boundary MUST disable persistent reuse. A real operation rebuilds
  1088	from the revalidated snapshot into newly established protected state; dry-run
  1089	reports `would-rebuild-untrusted-cache`; status is non-current. Self-consistent
  1090	receipt, marker, artifact, and hash bytes do not repair or authenticate an
  1091	untrusted boundary.
  1092	
  1093	The logical cache key, exact receipt bytes, artifact-relative path, artifact
  1094	bytes/hash/size, and validation outcomes are portable. Manager-home paths,
  1095	physical cache-root and driver-directory names, receipt filenames, lock and
  1096	quarantine names, and storage backends are implementation-specific. A manager
  1097	MUST NOT infer portable paths such as `build-cache` or `.csk-build.json`.
  1098	
  1099	Entries are built in operation-private staging and published atomically and
  1100	immutably under the manager-home mutation lock. Receipt hashes are deterministic
  1101	corruption/currentness identifiers, not signatures, MACs, attestations, or
  1102	proof of provenance. The verified protected-state boundary supplies local
  1103	provenance; registry audit continues to attest source, not the compiled result.
  1104	
  1105	Dry-run MAY obtain, raw-validate, hash, and independently audit an exact
  1106	external snapshot in removable operation-private state; MAY validate local
  1107	build-root exclusion; MAY compute build-source and trusted-toolchain identity;
  1108	and MAY inspect protected entries read-only. It MUST NOT invoke `go list`,
  1109	`go build`, a compiler, or linker; create Go caches, a persistent repository or
  1110	snapshot, an audit response, a persistent memo, a mutation lock or journal;
  1111	publish, quarantine, repair, or adopt cache bytes; copy build roots into
  1112	runtime; or mutate installation, registry, audit, marker, shim, adapter, or
  1113	consumer state. A claimed external cache hit requires exact snapshot admission
  1114	and audit first. After exact receipt validation, a hit MUST NOT run
  1115	source-aware Go commands.
  1116	
  1117	### 9.4 Protected external snapshots, deduplication, and garbage collection
  1118	
  1119	The protected external snapshot key MUST contain the complete effective
  1120	identity kind and value, effective object format, full effective commit, and
  1121	external `curator-build-source-v1` digest. A manager MUST NOT omit, replace, or
  1122	infer any key component from declared state. Snapshot bytes MAY be
  1123	deduplicated only when every component of the complete snapshot key is equal.
  1124	Audit decisions remain subject-specific and MUST NOT be deduplicated or reused
  1125	merely because snapshot bytes or snapshot keys are equal.
  1126	
  1127	Garbage collection MUST revalidate protected-cache boundaries and run under
  1128	the manager-home mutation lock. Its live roots MUST include:
  1129	
  1130	- for every valid marker-v3 installation, each referenced local skill raw
  1131	  snapshot, complete external frozen-snapshot key, receipt/artifact key, and
  1132	  manager-generated shim relationship;
  1133	- every staged or published snapshot and artifact referenced by an in-flight
  1134	  transaction journal and needed for commit or rollback; and
  1135	- the roots already defined for valid marker-v1 and marker-v2 installations,
  1136	  whose behavior remains unchanged.
  1137	
  1138	Receipt content alone MUST NOT make an entry live. If a marker or journal is
  1139	unreadable, or a cache boundary or possible reference cannot be proved,
  1140	garbage collection MUST conservatively retain the uncertain entries. It MUST
  1141	NOT execute or adopt source or artifact bytes, repair permissions to make
  1142	candidate bytes trusted, or infer liveness from an unvalidated partial record.
  1143	Physical snapshot, artifact, receipt, lock, journal, quarantine, and
  1144	garbage-collection paths remain implementation-specific.
  1145	
  1146	## 10. Install markers
  1147	
  1148	Every installed closure node has `.csk-install.json`. Managers supporting
  1149	schema 7 MUST read marker schemas 1, 2, and 3. They MUST write marker schema 2
  1150	for schema 1 through 6 installation mutations and marker schema 3 for schema 7
  1151	installation mutations. They MAY continue to regard a valid marker-v1
  1152	installation as current for a schema 1 through 5 package. Marker v1 and v2
  1153	retain their existing shapes and meanings.
  1154	
  1155	Marker v2 permits `skill_schema_version` through 6 and requires sorted
  1156	`build_roots` and a `builds` object, including empty values for installations
  1157	without active compiled commands. `build_source` is REQUIRED exactly when
  1158	`builds` is non-empty and MUST otherwise be absent. Each lexically ordered
  1159	`builds` entry records driver, logical cache key, `receipt_sha256`, artifact
  1160	SHA-256, and manager-derived artifact-relative path. A build shim MUST target
  1161	the immutable cache artifact selected by the effective plan and marker, not the
  1162	commit-keyed script runtime store.
  1163	
  1164	Marker v3 permits `skill_schema_version` through 7 and represents local-only,
  1165	external-only, and mixed command sets. Every build entry MUST explicitly record
  1166	its receipt schema version and its `execution_policy`, which MUST equal the
  1167	execution-policy identity inside the referenced receipt input. A local `go-v1`
  1168	entry MUST retain receipt schema 1 and marker-v2 entry semantics. An external
  1169	`go-repository-v1` entry MUST record receipt schema 2, repository identifier,
  1170	declared identity and immutable lock, OPTIONAL declared tag, effective identity,
  1171	object format and full commit, substitution state, external build-source
  1172	identity, descriptor target, logical cache key, `receipt_sha256`, artifact hash,
  1173	and manager-derived artifact path. No receipt or marker field MAY be inferred
  1174	from a driver name.
  1175	
  1176	A marker-v2 build record keeps its frozen schema-6 shape and does not gain an
  1177	`execution_policy` member. Its execution-policy binding is transitive and
  1178	complete: the recorded logical cache key and `receipt_sha256` are computed over
  1179	an input that contains the execution-policy identity, so a record written under
  1180	one execution contract can never validate against another.
  1181	
  1182	Marker-v3 top-level `build_source` and `build_roots` retain schema-6 meaning.
  1183	Top-level `build_source` is REQUIRED exactly when at least one active local
  1184	`go-v1` command exists and MUST otherwise be absent. External-only
  1185	installations MUST bind source per external build entry and MUST NOT use the
  1186	consuming skill's raw snapshot as external compiled-source identity. An
  1187	unsubstituted external entry MAY record declared tag only from a receipt whose
  1188	producing operation completed exact-tag acquisition and equality with the
  1189	immutable lock; the marker field is not independent proof.
  1190	
  1191	`locale` is always present and is a string or `null`. Required set-like arrays
  1192	are always arrays, including when empty.
  1193	
  1194	The following arrays are set-like and writers sort them by Unicode scalar
  1195	value: `agents`, `commands`, `dependencies`, `files`, `requirements`,
  1196	`runtime_roots`, `build_roots`, `requirers`, `activation.commands`, and every
  1197	MCP agent list. Writers sort `builds` keys by Unicode scalar value. Object
  1198	member order and whitespace are not significant.
  1199	
  1200	An installation is current only when the marker schema is supported; ref kind,
  1201	ref, commit, locale, agents, activation, substitution, MCP findings, and
  1202	attestation match the effective plan; and the installed content hash matches
  1203	`content_sha256`. For a build-enabled marker, currentness additionally requires
  1204	the declared local build roots and static context exclusion to match; every
  1205	available local or external protected snapshot's `build_source` to match the
  1206	effective plan and receipt input; each logical cache key and manager-derived
  1207	artifact path to match; and every referenced receipt/artifact to validate below
  1208	a currently verified manager-protected boundary.
  1209	
  1210	For external entries, currentness also requires declared and effective
  1211	identity, object format, full commit, target, and substitution state to match;
  1212	the external snapshot boundary and source digest to validate; the receipt
  1213	schema-2 relationship to match exactly; and the shim to select the protected
  1214	artifact. A missing snapshot that the marker claims should exist, prompt-
  1215	visible or runtime-copied external source, untrusted boundary, corrupt
  1216	receipt/artifact, wrong target or toolchain, or mismatched source identity
  1217	makes the installation non-current. When existing profile semantics
  1218	distinguish unavailable evidence from drift, a result MAY be `unknown`, but it
  1219	MUST NOT be current and a checking status command MUST return non-zero.
  1220	
  1221	Read-only status MUST NOT contact an external remote merely to retest tag
  1222	availability or movement. It MUST NOT fetch, repair, quarantine, alter
  1223	permissions, refresh a snapshot, invoke a compiler or signer, or execute an
  1224	artifact. Repair MUST reacquire, validate, and audit the exact effective source
  1225	and MUST rebuild into protected state rather than adopt candidate bytes. For an
  1226	unsubstituted tagged source, repair MUST use only the exact-tag path and MUST
  1227	reprove terminal equality with the immutable lock; an old snapshot or direct-
  1228	object fetch MUST NOT substitute for that assertion.
  1229	
  1230	Unsupported or unreadable markers are not current. A moved tag is a warning,
  1231	or an error under strict-tag policy.
  1232	
  1233	The marker and its referenced receipt hashes are not signatures and MUST NOT be
  1234	used as authorization tokens or provenance proof. Registry attestations are
  1235	reverified from signed records when fresh trust is required.
  1236	
  1237	## 11. Adapter ledger
  1238	
  1239	Every managed adapter root contains `.csk-managed.json` conforming to
  1240	`adapter-ledger-v1.schema.json`. `entries` is a sorted unique list of skill
  1241	names owned by the manager in that adapter root. A manager MUST remove only
  1242	entries in its preceding ledger and MUST fail rather than overwrite an
  1243	unmanaged conflicting entry.
  1244	
  1245	## 12. External build publication and closed extensions
  1246	
  1247	### 12.1 Publication, shims, and rollback
  1248	
  1249	All `go-repository-v1` compilation MUST occur in operation-private staging
  1250	before installation mutation. The manager MUST validate and hash the sole
  1251	manager-derived artifact, generate canonical receipt and marker bytes, and
  1252	publish immutable snapshots and artifacts only under the manager-home mutation
  1253	lock. The consumer ledger MUST commit last. A failure after publication begins
  1254	MUST roll committed targets back in reverse order while retaining that lock.
  1255	Pre-publication source, object, descriptor, audit, cache, or compiler failures
  1256	MUST leave live installation state unchanged.
  1257	
  1258	One manifest command key MAY select the same descriptor target as another
  1259	command key, but each command MUST derive its own artifact name, cache input,
  1260	receipt, and shim. The repository MUST NOT request aliases, sidecars,
  1261	post-build copies, or secondary PATH entries. A build shim MUST be manager-
  1262	owned and point exactly to the marker-selected protected artifact. It MUST NOT
  1263	point into a Git object database, frozen source snapshot, checkout, staging
  1264	directory, or commit-keyed script runtime. Structural validation MUST NOT use a
  1265	shell, executable lookup, or execution of the built output.
     1	# Decision 0004: compile-only build drivers
     2	
     3	## Context
     4	
     5	Schemas 1 through 5 can copy script commands or resolve system commands but
     6	cannot produce a native artifact from source. Allowing a package to supply a
     7	shell command, executable, argument vector, environment, plugin, generator, or
     8	build-system recipe would contradict the existing rule that installation does
     9	not execute package-provided code.
    10	
    11	Compiled artifacts also cannot reuse the existing `skill + commit` runtime
    12	identity. Compiler-visible inputs include files outside one package directory,
    13	the selected native target and policy, and the exact trusted toolchain. An
    14	internally consistent receipt from an attacker-writable cache is not proof that
    15	the manager produced its artifact.
    16	
    17	## Decision
    18	
    19	Manifest schema 6 is a declarative compiled-artifact extension. It adds
    20	explicit `build_roots` and one closed command object containing only
    21	`type: "build"`, `driver: "go-v1"`, and `source_dir`. Build roots are dedicated
    22	link-free module roots below the snapshot, cannot be `.`, cannot overlap
    23	runtime roots, and are statically excluded from agent context and runtime
    24	copying before cache lookup or compiler discovery. The same exclusion applies
    25	to real builds, exact cache hits, and dry-runs. Artifacts live only in
    26	manager-owned staging and immutable cache state.
    27	
    28	Protocol v1 defines only `go-v1`. It is a fixed native, vendor-only,
    29	networkless, cgo-free Go 1.23+ pipeline using an operator-trusted and
    30	fingerprinted release family. The manager owns the process graph, environment,
    31	arguments, target, output path, dependency preflight, directive checks, and
    32	internal-link policy. It rejects non-standard assembly, host objects,
    33	`//go:cgo_import_dynamic`, external linking, libgcc fallback, PGO, workspaces,
    34	and toolchain switching, and it starts no program outside the fingerprinted Go
    35	launcher and its fingerprinted tool directory. The built artifact is never
    36	executed during manager activity.
    37	
    38	**Superseded before publication.** The original clause required the manager to
    39	invoke the fingerprinted Go launcher as its own direct child. That graph left no
    40	place to install controls before compiler code runs. Since neither `1.0.0-rc.4`
    41	nor `1.0.0-rc.5` has been released or pinned, the process graph is amended in
    42	place rather than deprecated: one identity-verified manager-owned worker sits
    43	between the manager parent and the fingerprinted Go launcher, and the operation
    44	declares the named execution policy `manager-worker-v1`. See decision 0006.
    45	The process-graph rule is manager selection plus identity verification, not a
    46	kernel executable allowlist, and the offline `network: "none"` policy is fixed Go
    47	module, proxy, checksum-database, and VCS configuration rather than kernel
    48	network denial; `protocol/core.md` section 4.2.1 names both boundaries and the
    49	guarantees deferred to `STORY-260728-327soo`. Everything else in this decision,
    50	including the exact argument vectors, the environment, the rejection matrix, the
    51	manager-derived output, and the no-execution rule, is unchanged.
    52	
    53	Two source/currentness identities remain separate:
    54	
    55	- installed-tree `content_sha256` retains its schema 1 through 5 semantics and
    56	  excludes the manager marker;
    57	- `curator-build-source-v1` is domain-separated and length-framed over every
    58	  regular file in the validated raw snapshot, including a package-provided
    59	  root `.csk-install.json`, and is computed before cache lookup.
    60	
    61	The build input also binds `curator-go-toolchain-v1`, command/source selection,
    62	native target and tuning, driver revision, the execution-policy identity, and
    63	the complete fixed policy. Its CCJ-1 digest is the logical cache key. Exact canonical receipt bytes bind the
    64	input and manager-derived artifact path/hash/size; their digest supplies
    65	corruption and currentness detection, not authentication. Persistent reuse
    66	requires a separately verified manager-created, owner-protected, link-safe
    67	cache boundary. Untrusted protection forces a rebuild from the revalidated
    68	snapshot, even when every embedded hash matches.
    69	
    70	Marker schema 2 records build roots, build-source identity, logical cache keys,
    71	receipt hashes, artifact hashes, and manager-derived artifact-relative paths.
    72	Managers read marker schemas 1 and 2, write v2 after mutation, and may retain a
    73	valid v1 marker as current for schema 1 through 5 packages. Shims for build
    74	commands select immutable cache artifacts rather than the commit-keyed script
    75	runtime store.
    76	
    77	A real operation validates and builds misses in operation-private staging
    78	before installation mutation. Independent projects may validate and compile
    79	concurrently, but cache publication, project/global/hybrid/adapter mutation,
    80	consumer update, rollback/recovery, and garbage collection serialize beneath
    81	one manager-home mutation lock. One journal covers the ordered mutable target
    82	set; the consumer ledger commits last; rollback stays locked and proceeds in
    83	reverse commit order. This prevents cross-project lost updates and stale
    84	backup restoration.
    85	
    86	Dry-run performs only package-independent toolchain probes and read-only
    87	planning. It does not run `go list`, `go build`, a compiler, or linker; create
    88	Go caches, persistent fingerprint memos, mutation locks, journals, or manager
    89	state; publish/quarantine/repair cache entries; or change agent context,
    90	runtime, markers, shims, adapters, audit/registry state, or consumers.
    91	
    92	Only logical identity and validation are portable. Manager-home directories,
    93	physical cache-root and driver-subdirectory names, receipt filenames, lock and
    94	quarantine names, and storage backends are deliberately non-normative. An
    95	illustrative layout such as `<home>/<cache>/go-v1/<key>/bin/<command>` MUST NOT
    96	be treated as a compatibility identifier.
    97	
    98	## Rejected alternatives
    99	
   100	- Arbitrary package shell commands, executable names, argv/environment arrays,
   101	  output selection, hooks, plugins, generators, tests, and pre/post-build steps
   102	  were rejected because they transfer install-time execution control to the
   103	  package.
   104	- Cargo was rejected for v1 because build scripts and procedural macros execute
   105	  package-selected code. Direct Rust compilation still needs a closed
   106	  dependency and linking contract.
   107	- Zig and Swift were deferred because their build manifests, plugins, macros,
   108	  or compile-time execution require distinct process and side-effect models.
   109	- Make, CMake, Ninja, and Meson were rejected as package-controlled recipe
   110	  frontends rather than fixed compilers.
   111	- Maven, Gradle, MSBuild, and package-oriented Java/Kotlin/.NET compilation were
   112	  deferred because tasks, plugins, annotation processors, analyzers, and source
   113	  generators execute at build time.
   114	- npm lifecycle scripts and Node/TypeScript bundler configuration, loaders, and
   115	  plugins were rejected. A fixed transpiler or Deno subset still needs a closed
   116	  dependency, configuration, and runtime-output model.
   117	- Python package builds were rejected because PEP 517 frontends execute the
   118	  package-selected backend and may resolve dynamic build requirements.
   119	- Direct C/C++ compilers were deferred because a portable driver must own and
   120	  identify source enumeration, preprocessing, sysroots, response files,
   121	  compiler plugins, native dependencies, and linking inputs.
   122	- `skill + commit`, installed-tree `content_sha256`, and receipt-only trust were
   123	  rejected as cache identities because they omit compiler/toolchain policy or
   124	  allow attacker-created self-consistent candidates.
   125	- Standardizing cache directory names and receipt filenames was rejected
   126	  because machine-local layout is an implementation boundary, not wire state.
   127	
   128	## Compatibility impact
   129	
   130	Schema 1 through 5 manifests and marker-v1 currentness retain their exact
   131	deployed behavior. They reject the schema-6-only field and command surface.
   132	Schema 6 and marker v2 are explicit reader/writer version transitions; readers
   133	never infer them from fields. The build feature is released with a new current
   134	protocol `1.0.0-rc.4` conformance claim schema 2. Conformance claim schema 1
   135	remains frozen as historical rc.3 evidence and cannot claim schema-6 or
   136	build-driver behavior.
   137	
   138	## Security impact
   139	
   140	The accepted model preserves the no-hooks invariant while admitting untrusted
   141	compiler input. It does not claim that the Go compiler or compiled artifact is
   142	safe. Compiler-input denial of service and vulnerabilities require host
   143	resource limits and sandboxing; decision 0006 defines how much of that the
   144	portable profile actually delivers and what remains deferred. Artifact execution
   145	remains a later user action. Protected local cache state is part of the v1 trusted computing base;
   146	same-principal, administrator/root, kernel, and hostile-storage compromise are
   147	outside that boundary and require cache reuse to be disabled when unacceptable.
   148	
   149	## Future-driver rule
   150	
   151	A future driver requires a new closed identifier and independent protocol and
   152	security review. The proposal must define a strict manifest surface, complete
   153	compiler-input boundary, trusted toolchain identity, fixed direct process graph
   154	and environment, network/dependency/link policy, manager-derived output,
   155	logical cache/receipt identity, dry-run behavior, lifecycle/rollback rules, and
   156	cross-language conformance vectors. It MUST demonstrate that package-selected
   157	hooks, plugins, generators, macros, build recipes, and produced output cannot
   158	execute during manager activity. No future driver may be enabled by broadening
   159	`go-v1` or adding a generic fallback.

